## USAMO 2024 Problem 2 — Solution

**Answer: 50**

### Lower Bound

Assume $n \ge 50$ (for $n < 50$ the problem is trivial). Take $T = \{S_1, \dots, S_n\}$, so $|T| = n$. The hypothesis gives
$$n \;\Big|\; \left|\bigcap_{i=1}^{n} S_i\right|.$$
Since the intersection is non-empty, $\left|\bigcap_{i=1}^n S_i\right| \ge n \ge 50$. Every element in this full intersection lies in all $n \ge 50$ sets, hence is in at least 50 sets. Therefore **at least 50 elements** are in $\ge 50$ sets.

### Upper Bound — Construction with $n=50$

We build a symmetric configuration on $n=50$ sets indexed by $[50]$, achieving exactly 50 such elements.

For each subset $U\subseteq[50]$, let $h(U)$ = number of elements whose **support** (indices $i$ with $x\in S_i$) is exactly $U$. We choose $h(U)$ to depend only on $|U|=k$, writing it as $h(k)$.

**Set $h(50)=50$.** For $k = 49,48,\dots,1$, define recursively
$$h(k) \;=\; \left(-\,\sum_{j=k+1}^{50}\binom{50-k}{j-k}\,h(j)\right)\bmod k \;\in\;\{0,1,\dots,k-1\}.$$

For each $U\subseteq[50]$ with $|U|=k$, create $h(k)$ **distinct** integers, each placed in exactly the sets $\{S_i : i\in U\}$.

### Verification

For any $T\subseteq[50]$ with $|T|=k\ge 1$:
$$\left|\bigcap_{i\in T}S_i\right| \;=\; \sum_{j=k}^{50}\binom{50-k}{j-k}\,h(j) \;=\; h(k)+\sum_{j=k+1}^{50}\binom{50-k}{j-k}\,h(j).$$
By construction, $h(k)\equiv -\sum_{j=k+1}^{50}\binom{50-k}{j-k}h(j)\pmod{k}$, so the intersection size is divisible by $k$. ✓

The full intersection ($k=50$) has size $h(50)=50>0$, so it is non-empty. ✓

**Count of elements in $\ge 50$ sets:** Only elements with support size $\ge 50$ qualify. Since $n=50$, this means support size exactly $50$, giving
$$\binom{50}{50}\cdot h(50) = 1\cdot 50 = 50.$$
All other elements have support size $\le 49 < 50$ and do not count.

### Conclusion

The lower bound of $50$ (forced by the full intersection) matches the construction achieving exactly $50$.

$$\boxed{50}$$

